+
+### Global Illumination Caching
+A technique that pre-computes global illumination for each voxel using path tracing and stores it in a volume, which is then used during ray marching for real-time rendering [S3]. The global illumination of each voxel is computed with path tracing and stored in a global illumination volume [S3]. Both the global illumination volume and local illumination contribution are taken into account during the ray marching step when accumulating the illumination along the ray [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Pre-computation:** The pre-computation phase is expensive, as it requires evaluating lighting for every voxel in the cache domain. The complexity is proportional to the number of voxels in the cache times the number of light sources and the cost of shadow ray marching [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Rendering:** The rendering phase is significantly faster than standard per-sample shading because it replaces shadow ray marching with a single texture lookup per sample [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory:** The method requires additional memory for the illumination cache texture. The size depends on the cache resolution and the number of channels stored (e.g., RGB or scalar) [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   Evaluated on an NVIDIA RTX 3500 Ada GPU with a chunked Kolumbo dataset (5.9 billion voxels, chunked into nine sub-volumes of 512x935x1501 voxels) [S1, unofficial].
-*   The method achieves speedups of over 45 times compared to dense sampling methods [S2].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Enables real-time rendering of complex lighting in large volumes; reduces per-frame computational cost significantly [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-computation time; assumes isotropic scattering; suffers from lighting artifacts at chunk borders when large volumes are chunked; limited by maximum texture dimensions in some graphics APIs (e.g., DirectX 11/12 limit of 2048 voxels per dimension) [S1, unofficial].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   Use when real-time performance is critical and the volume exhibits approximately isotropic scattering.
-*   Suitable for large datasets where per-sample shadow ray marching is too slow.
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   Do not use if the volume exhibits strong anisotropic scattering.
-*   Avoid if the volume cannot be chunked without unacceptable lighting artifacts at borders.
-*   Avoid if the hardware does not support large "true" volume textures or if the required cache size exceeds available VRAM.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Pre-computation is $O(N_{cache} \cdot N_{samples})$, where $N_{cache}$ is the number of voxels in the cache and $N_{samples}$ is the number of samples per shadow ray or light path [S1, unofficial]. Rendering is $O(N_{pixels} \cdot N_{steps})$, with a constant-time lighting lookup per step, significantly faster than per-sample lighting [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{cache})$ for the illumination cache texture [S1, unofficial]. The cache size is independent of the number of light sources, unlike per-sample lighting which scales with light count [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Evaluated on an NVIDIA RTX 3500 Ada GPU with a chunked Kolumbo dataset (5.9 billion voxels, chunked into nine sub-volumes of 512x935x1501 voxels) [S1, unofficial]. The plugin allows for beautiful rendering with great performance on a single machine, though VRAM is a limiting factor [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Drastically improves rendering performance by eliminating per-sample lighting calculations [S1, unofficial]. Suitable for real-time rendering of large volume datasets [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires significant pre-computation time [S1, unofficial]. Chunking leads to lighting artifacts at boundaries [S1, unofficial]. Limited by maximum texture dimensions in some APIs (e.g., DirectX 11/12 limit of 2048 voxels per dimension) [S1, unofficial]. Not suitable for dynamic light sources or volumes that change frequently [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The illumination caching technique described here is based on an implementation by Sundén and Ropinski [16], which is used in the TBRayMarcher plugin for Unreal Engine [S1, unofficial]. The plugin was evaluated in the context of rendering large volume datasets in Unreal Engine 5 [S1, unofficial]. The underlying assumption of isotropic scattering allows the lighting distribution to be pre-computed and stored, a concept related to radiance caching methods such as those by Jarosz et al. [S2].
+The technique is based on the implementation by Sundén and Ropinski [S1, unofficial]. It is used in the TBRayMarcher Plugin for Unreal Engine [S1, unofficial]. The method is particularly relevant for medical datasets and large volume datasets where real-time performance is required [S1, unofficial]. The approach is also related to radiance caching [S2] and global illumination caching [S3] in the context of participating media and combined volume/surface rendering.
 
